#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092083 :question/id #uuid "e405bf60-d500-55df-90be-a938dc9dfc93" :question/math-academy-id "e-6080" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^3 - x^2$ at the point $(1,0)$ is given by $y=px+q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the values of $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^3 - x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2})=3{x}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1, 0){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=3-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =-(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635 :topic/id #uuid "5a901d31-08d1-5602-a6b3-9ae8fa80845b" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068694}] :topic/math-academy-id 418 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049840}] :topic/title "Equations of Lines in Point-Slope Form"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760606 :question-generator/id #uuid "d19eea52-a786-5b60-9dc8-262702b85ad0" :question-generator/script "generators/2d/2dad35d505965ce58f224701b7f4ca5db306cc23475610593ecb06f7f76b9908/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3826d54-f39e-5e45-982c-0196c96b30c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090533} {:answer/id #uuid "bf822091-623a-5b75-a700-06562c3fa7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090534} {:answer/id #uuid "c014a862-aa95-5ee5-8cc4-90a1115cf633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090535} {:answer/id #uuid "e535f72e-5b43-5878-a978-9a20e1d8470d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090536} {:answer/id #uuid "48a27df3-0a04-52a4-9003-fd2c61c07e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf822091-623a-5b75-a700-06562c3fa7b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090534} :answer-field/id #uuid "7907bce1-6e91-548c-bf8c-f50341390d5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090532}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a649b33-83ae-5424-9852-7d09d082fffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-47406" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $(2,8)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})=4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(2)=4(2)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-8 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ 8y-64 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+8y & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=8+66=74.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092724 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355be2d9-4b88-5b77-9339-b936d34c03d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327396} {:answer/id #uuid "464056c0-a0f2-5bfe-b408-9aa941897b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327549} {:answer/id #uuid "877dfb13-75fb-5a2c-9893-61ca31652033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328100} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e2320c-32e8-55f4-858e-5dbe27b873eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328534} {:answer/id #uuid "beaa42aa-dbd3-5b01-b81b-d37b04fb64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "464056c0-a0f2-5bfe-b408-9aa941897b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327549} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf413bf-6c88-5e40-a13e-f1edd13516cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329596}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1cac491-67f0-58ca-a542-a3096fac9ba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-47408" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{5}-2x$ at the point $(1,0)$ is given by $y=ax+b$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants. Find the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-2x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{5}-2x)=10{x}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=10(1)^{4}-2=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f08372b9-b9c4-55c6-8130-e0b8b3702f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f08372b9-b9c4-55c6-8130-e0b8b3702f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232834} :answer-field/id #uuid "db38cb75-4a2a-5faa-9624-427353b91e8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232833} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b547da1-c70a-5896-a34a-6ec75b964807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b547da1-c70a-5896-a34a-6ec75b964807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232836} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd6f2a88-c9b9-5995-986d-d685474cd66d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232835}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a4f8ec-9201-5937-9164-8f60e8aa0551" :question/math-academy-id "q-340942" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ at the point $(2,9).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+1.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+1)=4x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,9):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=4(2)=8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ 8y-72 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+8\\cdot y & =74.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040d200c-28e8-5295-aed7-d520e9cd3e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "040d200c-28e8-5295-aed7-d520e9cd3e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232867} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bbf44b5-3a8a-56c7-b5b8-1662b361ece7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232866} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cd55b4-a689-56b0-ad87-45efc20ee812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03cd55b4-a689-56b0-ad87-45efc20ee812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232869} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed007c73-3599-5c78-af4d-81875dcfb067" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232868}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31017300-66b4-5dbf-aba1-d07ddfcf96a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342163" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=4x-{x}^{3}$ at the point $(1,3).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4x-{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-{x}^{3})=4-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=4-3({1}^{2})=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-1\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+1\\cdot y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234077 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a936e865-c9ea-5dbc-8fe2-541cf4f4a61c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47405" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,16)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})=6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,16):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{'}(2)=6(2)^{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{'}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(16) & =-\\frac{1}{24}(x-(2)) \\\\ 24y-384 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+24y & =386.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q-p=386-24=362.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1ba0de-62d4-5250-bb47-4f2a59dfcd01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b1ba0de-62d4-5250-bb47-4f2a59dfcd01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234782} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c9641fb-c8f3-5eae-84db-0c0deb61bedb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235032}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dde111b-f4d3-5f08-91c6-efd9593de57f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167327" :question/problem "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$\\,y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4decc47b-808c-50a3-a38e-a4db1547ccf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4decc47b-808c-50a3-a38e-a4db1547ccf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234665} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a661154-3b70-50d8-a6af-d295b9a56bb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234996}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d970612-b8f0-51a2-9d52-e3fa3b8f917e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167781" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$\\,y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc92670-1ee0-5a94-8633-ae4dbac0d967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236503} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4214d-961d-587a-99f1-77840683d122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236888} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b8f9a0-77e0-5918-ba96-c076ccd0a4a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237149} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b35714-a77b-5cf7-b620-bbbde6e0b91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237187} {:answer/id #uuid "f71cabe8-d7a8-53fa-97ce-f3e2f21c3251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9f4214d-961d-587a-99f1-77840683d122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236888} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fc67540-c6a4-5c72-ab19-c7578f38fabe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237455}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf6fcd75-6ae3-55a9-acc1-22b5660350be" :question/math-academy-id "q-103546" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(1,4)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{3}}}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ 2y-8 & =x-1 \\\\ x-2y & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q-p=-7-(-2)=-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8aacf8a-923a-5706-a78c-108e65099a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8aacf8a-923a-5706-a78c-108e65099a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313826} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ac9af9-f5be-5aac-9b98-a73bb56de950" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314252} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f398213-9b43-5009-a712-5e39a192aab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f398213-9b43-5009-a712-5e39a192aab2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312352} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e679c8-0512-5ea7-a995-5f08819c8352" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314554}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6db0058b-56f3-5a30-bae9-0cf86c420390" :question/math-academy-id "q-340945" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(2,2).$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-16{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{16}{(2)^{3}}=-\\frac{16}{8}=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y-4 & =x-2 \\\\ x-2\\cdot y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8672281f-8981-5889-ae04-fc0ebe51ce35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8672281f-8981-5889-ae04-fc0ebe51ce35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315875} :answer-field/id #uuid "076abe3c-2890-583c-ace8-ff9d99106670" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a195177d-1813-5a16-ae86-c4c7cd7b18a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a195177d-1813-5a16-ae86-c4c7cd7b18a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316028} :answer-field/id #uuid "9edf45fe-a749-5a58-b21a-dc03a59337f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316914}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7217f3b7-79ba-5f9c-b811-2d79787fbad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342162" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=6{x}^{2}-7x+4$ at the point $(0,4).$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-7x+4.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-7x+4)=12x-7\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,4):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(0)=12(0)-7=-7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{7}(x-0) \\\\ 7(y-4) & =x \\\\ 7y-28 & =x \\\\ x-7\\cdot y & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14739741-a199-5044-95db-68e615cfe350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14739741-a199-5044-95db-68e615cfe350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315242} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c1804f-c3c3-527f-ad52-389c7f415fc7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef369bbf-3dd9-544a-b45c-a35adc1c8bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef369bbf-3dd9-544a-b45c-a35adc1c8bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316448} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5cbe1d4-2ba5-517a-85ba-01b462393fb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316932}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "225f0ba1-57bb-5f34-91e4-9ca304d64d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342168" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,-6).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}.\n$$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}\\right)=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}-\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-6):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}({2}^{5})-\\frac{21}{4}({2}^{2})=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ -3(y+6) & =x-2 \\\\ -3y-18 & =x-2 \\\\ x+3\\cdot y & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eaf6866-59c7-5bcf-85b0-5e039d9cf80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eaf6866-59c7-5bcf-85b0-5e039d9cf80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315568} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee849c8-86a4-5fad-acfb-56e96048a591" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901ca621-ed5f-5750-a913-063b18972900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "901ca621-ed5f-5750-a913-063b18972900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315924} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a60e86-2a15-5a46-a72a-b8277699810a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316981}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "773759d8-9f76-5cef-8ffc-9fa90fd16743" :question/math-academy-id "q-342190" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}\\right).$\n\n$8x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $\\left(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}\\right):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{2}}}=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{3}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\ 16\\left(y-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) & =16\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(x-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8\\left(x-\\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8x+1 \\\\ 8x+16\\cdot y & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e1490f-8a2f-5aa1-b46d-abffe2ecf67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327216} {:answer/id #uuid "2a251a8f-d542-5932-929c-cdccb0e5eaa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327299} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2eccf0-2aa7-5272-b036-0190d7bed899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328001} {:answer/id #uuid "906c08ad-b6aa-508c-9085-351bcf96badc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328194} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f5f70f-61df-5d3d-b04a-f2eee1395813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a251a8f-d542-5932-929c-cdccb0e5eaa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327299} :answer-field/id #uuid "24cc6f6b-5575-52a5-a07a-afc26dd7d423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329250}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abc350e7-3687-5b2a-9ff1-fa33c7a23d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-47409" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-x$ at the point $(1,3)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p-q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-x\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-2}-x) \\\\  & =-8{x}^{-3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-8(1)^{-3}-1=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-1) \\\\ 9y-27 & =x-1 \\\\ x-9y & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p-q=-9-(-26)=17.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10131252-4695-56ed-8c59-747e545f594c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392584} {:answer/id #uuid "e6984861-e3cb-5365-9e9e-1ecd4311dcb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392585} {:answer/id #uuid "859687f9-c59b-551f-aaa5-e7e3c04ba6c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392586} {:answer/id #uuid "95424aa7-6153-5c48-9071-e6e5ac4c3b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392587} {:answer/id #uuid "41016988-d005-506a-b687-2940e1c88cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95424aa7-6153-5c48-9071-e6e5ac4c3b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392587} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9549a2f-75a6-5fa7-b3e3-19c27b3b484f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392583}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "414ee72f-747c-573a-9cad-a9cbdad6101a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47410" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+4x$ at the point $(1,7)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+4x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+4x)=6x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=6(1)+4=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(7) & =-\\frac{1}{10}(x-1) \\\\ 10y-70 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+10y & =71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=10+71=81.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190621cf-ca9e-5595-8816-3501bc000442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x-\\pi}{2e^{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "190621cf-ca9e-5595-8816-3501bc000442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x-\\pi}{2e^{\\pi}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513597} :answer-field/id #uuid "059506ee-d597-56af-bfce-bf17819d6958" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514211}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "745a29fd-db69-5896-9d5f-b9b3dcaa95b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-166074" :question/problem "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 4, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df0f97f-4f39-5b1c-b10c-447e782fc12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523284} {:answer/id #uuid "6dae38db-68cf-5488-8070-c06480732642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523477} {:answer/id #uuid "81b9bb21-5627-5934-b71d-4ea33aa8e42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523530} {:answer/id #uuid "c53b0336-4b5b-5bc7-a56d-5493c7cb1d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523668} {:answer/id #uuid "f64ac374-af28-5718-ae03-405b4bfb5794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0df0f97f-4f39-5b1c-b10c-447e782fc12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523284} :answer-field/id #uuid "6769aa7f-4e0f-5ed2-acb1-f447e8594699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523840}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77bfba8d-0add-517b-b669-b916e6dff897" :question/math-academy-id "q-47412" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ at the point $(1,3)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p-q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-9{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{9}{(1)^{4}}=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-1) \\\\ 9y-27 & =x-1 \\\\ x-9y & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p-q=-9-(-26)=17.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e827c707-4183-5bda-8490-ae6373ae9ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e827c707-4183-5bda-8490-ae6373ae9ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546771} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa428138-3fdd-52e3-90d7-e57b79a7836a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85168c9f-eca2-5f20-86c3-38ab8f99e07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85168c9f-eca2-5f20-86c3-38ab8f99e07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546543} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1fb6fa9-d25d-5930-b90b-ff3334996e24" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546939}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d27eb9e3-1a36-5293-b88b-ba67a6d55870" :question/math-academy-id "q-342187" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=4x-\\frac{2}{x}$ at the point $(-1,-2).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4x-\\frac{2}{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(4x-\\frac{2}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-2{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4+2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =4+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(-1)=4+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=4+2=6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{6}[x-(-1)] \\\\ -6(y+2) & =x+1 \\\\ -6y-12 & =x+1 \\\\ x+6\\cdot y & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0d57a8a-8cc2-5e2e-b732-91ab7b590574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0d57a8a-8cc2-5e2e-b732-91ab7b590574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578654} :answer-field/id #uuid "18483c38-3053-519d-b88d-7489fff9750a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578695}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83558817-3486-5c78-a29c-1e7dcf477ccf" :question/math-academy-id "q-167317" :question/problem "The equation of the normal to the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0\\cdot f(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is $m=3.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186587300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8eca89-2395-55b3-8252-76238752450d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586813} {:answer/id #uuid "5e42b7b0-4041-5c26-9b94-546174204f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586841} {:answer/id #uuid "aecf459b-65e3-5680-8845-208b9d4e6625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586987} {:answer/id #uuid "f4f3d460-07d6-5c46-bcc8-9ffa453ab5ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186587136} {:answer/id #uuid "ff970104-a210-598b-b724-6dd37e45dfb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186587149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e42b7b0-4041-5c26-9b94-546174204f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186586841} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a36f5e-c603-5506-b757-49e93673d742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186587264}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39e13271-c077-5d98-869c-5ff0144176c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-47398" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{x}$ at the point $(1,4)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{4}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m &={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(4) & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-(1)) \\\\ 4y-16 & =x-1 \\\\ x-4y & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=-4+(-15)=-19.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68744066-7973-54e1-b23e-715f345fe00e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68744066-7973-54e1-b23e-715f345fe00e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600588} :answer-field/id #uuid "42eaaaa8-abea-5991-8d78-54726e810f0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600722}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b15c61da-86e3-52e5-8ae8-f5e24550a63b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167770" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is\n\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0\\cdot f(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=x\\cdot f(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ is $m=3.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d34deb-6874-5f9a-bd79-08e923356267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30d34deb-6874-5f9a-bd79-08e923356267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710390} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad75ea8-d6b6-5b59-8a7a-e643fee608a3" :answer-field/key "constant" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7fb8330-854f-530b-9340-5d518c47d43d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7fb8330-854f-530b-9340-5d518c47d43d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710398} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d00056c-f84a-5be4-ae4d-d0a0b6e4192d" :answer-field/key "coefficient" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710388}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03b50b4a-0238-5d91-81cc-ea0543712da9" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^3+x+1$ at $(1,3)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^3+x+1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=3x^2+1.\n$$\n\nAt $(1,3)$ the tangent slope is $f'(1)=4$, so the normal slope is $-\\frac{1}{4}$. Use point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-3&=-\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)\\\\\n4y-12&=-x+1\\\\\nx+4y&=13.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first blank is $4$ and the second is $13$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f783bc65-5c7b-5fe7-b29d-06e0105bf9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710399} {:answer/id #uuid "839c37a1-bef4-4573-9e72-4833e06d0074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186760409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f783bc65-5c7b-5fe7-b29d-06e0105bf9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710399} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1526d5-2280-5f5b-adc5-e03a88b6fa76" :answer-field/key "intercept" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710395} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37008dbf-987e-5fcf-832c-6c3a6daa2fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710392} {:answer/id #uuid "5536419b-b39a-44bd-a721-5ea15b1234e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186760410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37008dbf-987e-5fcf-832c-6c3a6daa2fc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710392} :answer-field/id #uuid "f86790f1-2358-5b38-98cd-f49fdc826655" :answer-field/key "slope" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710400}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "338c0ca7-aea5-5baf-ba88-43d577ec3ad2" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to $y=x^2$ at $(1,1)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}" :question/worked-solution "The tangent slope is\n\n$$\nf'(1)=2(1)=2.\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so its slope is\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the point $(1,1)$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-1&=-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first blank is $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the second is $\\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nThe lesson diagram shows the normal as a dashed line through the same point, perpendicular to the tangent.\n\n![Curve with tangent and dashed normal at (1,1)](images/31/31ba09ff14e5bd485794f283c5d6a5b087b6c21fdb9242af01b75ec8e8744e0d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca6073c1-8b4a-57b6-b1cd-9945d16b094d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca6073c1-8b4a-57b6-b1cd-9945d16b094d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710396} :answer-field/id #uuid "14b1aca9-b70c-56f6-9e43-fafe7e9ed502" :answer-field/key "slope" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710389} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4b94bea-73da-5d00-885c-cbced9dffce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{241}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4b94bea-73da-5d00-885c-cbced9dffce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{241}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710397} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4aaf4e-01a8-5d44-96ce-e7e93f9bb9e9" :answer-field/key "intercept" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710394}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d7c9c49-19d3-52b0-87ac-8a31e56af5e6" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{x}$ at $\\left(4,\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}" :question/worked-solution "Rewrite the function with powers before differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=2x^{-1/2}+x^{-1}\\\\\nf'(x)&=-x^{-3/2}-x^{-2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at $x=4$:\n\n$$\nf'(4)=-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{16}=-\\frac{3}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe normal slope is the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{-3/16}=\\frac{16}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe normal passes through $\\left(4,\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\frac{5}{4}&=\\frac{16}{3}(x-4)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{16}{3}x-\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}\\\\\ny&=\\frac{16}{3}x-\\frac{241}{12}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $a=\\frac{16}{3}$ and $b=-\\frac{241}{12}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bbbd321-d2b4-54dd-b526-5a1fb0dd47ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bbbd321-d2b4-54dd-b526-5a1fb0dd47ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710839} :answer-field/id #uuid "af6bc1a6-83c9-520e-9f27-452ef54ed170" :answer-field/key "coefficient" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710844} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d093095-9be5-5539-b0cd-53bad9351f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d093095-9be5-5539-b0cd-53bad9351f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710840} :answer-field/id #uuid "f720bf51-849e-529c-ad3a-3f3bbf01a13a" :answer-field/key "constant" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710851}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2435fcf5-d549-504a-9f79-f1a9c6886cd6" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- \\frac{x^{6}}{8} + x^{3} - 1$ at $\\left(2,-1\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $x+ay=b$.\n\n$a=$ {{coefficient}}\n\n$b=$ {{constant}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=- \\frac{x^{6}}{8} + x^{3} - 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- \\frac{3 x^{5}}{4} + 3 x^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(2\\right)=- \\frac{3 \\left(2\\right)^{5}}{4} + 3 \\left(2\\right)^{2}=-12\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-12\\right)}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-1\\right)&=\\frac{1}{12}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{12}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)+\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{12} - \\frac{7}{6}\\\\\n\\left(-12\\right)y&=-x+\\left(2\\right)+\\left(12\\right)\\\\\nx+\\left(-12\\right)y&=14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient is $-12$ and the constant is $14$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186710841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268080bc-ba49-553b-8791-0b460b44d80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186710831}