C.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b99ad62bfedf6945020d345ef0bbc3531c58af1088698a78daf482cd4c14fe1.png)\n\nNote that in $△OBC,$ we have:\n\n- $BC=4|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=4\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}+4\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{b}+4\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5e92f9d-c8dc-5823-afa8-eae130a1ba43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364882} {:answer/id #uuid "3de53034-0a04-5d26-b49f-128745f4ea0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364883} {:answer/id #uuid "f76caff6-871d-509a-b53c-70f664edb2f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364884} {:answer/id #uuid "8993d132-186e-5f70-b539-0ecadbfbfd21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364885} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3c80c-11f7-505d-a02f-11ce6326b3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd3c80c-11f7-505d-a02f-11ce6326b3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364886} :answer-field/id #uuid "af541ef0-9d16-554c-878d-707d965733bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364881}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49639a9a-7a73-58c8-8e2e-c3ff76e8e2ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-120250" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1391a86176b1453c05f6ab43f2b5360fac00287e621d8917a142116990688b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/eadc3cc79d5c0f9b58bd8afc6864dca8e5f794cf398921e2ad540b491819562f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271316bb-de9b-5d8b-bad0-ffd1bfc5b101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} {:answer/id #uuid "633d97e3-8d84-57c9-8ac7-bdd2378d9d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420449} {:answer/id #uuid "c2051bdd-22cb-509c-8fb6-340e90eb858d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420456} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4325a-2b33-5c98-a020-248e21f996f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420459} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d19e05-07c5-5adc-9a1c-131ee586eacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "271316bb-de9b-5d8b-bad0-ffd1bfc5b101" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f94aaa-a8cf-5aa4-9f93-884e35c10a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420464}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b667d36f-710b-5326-b636-9597ec1ec3f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-77230" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cf7d0520b1d0654d5749651595acd48b7a08f2b2d55142b18ca63b2ded20f55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9e/9edea1e3ad727d11d7bfac884f27c1b435233fa7df561dbe2a4f3af45ea16bfa.png)\n\nIn $△OBC$, we have:\n\n- $BC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=3\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=2\\mathbf{b}+\\lambda \\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a}$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0730b596-e8ba-5761-8d72-995459b9f482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572322} {:answer/id #uuid "326a02a6-28fe-5680-bcbd-26768b11a79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572374} {:answer/id #uuid "5de4b890-ee0f-528b-8086-4f0f53ba2eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572429} {:answer/id #uuid "65dbfb77-dae6-5079-bb06-2fd6cdbe211b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572436} {:answer/id #uuid "89ca97dd-07f7-5d11-a1c6-2ec6ea64ebb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89ca97dd-07f7-5d11-a1c6-2ec6ea64ebb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572463} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13d9e16-1b93-5d32-9389-dc37d592577d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572647}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "049a751e-712d-54cc-ad6d-c6e3564444d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-120247" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2ea6acbf6574e970a798100729de9a92874bca90b8153db9883daa1cd6d98c2a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c31e9b0d926de6a3f8c9f35bd816349d6488c8b98864d8576fdbfc395bbd92d.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}=3\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57534557-824c-57ce-9724-420aeeed76a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57534557-824c-57ce-9724-420aeeed76a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572420} :answer-field/id #uuid "f419c0b9-3927-593f-9535-801465b684b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572659}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63f90954-aef6-5358-9e26-c4da4dbb21fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-327052" :question/problem "![](images/22/22f309ffc9ff6ae4a43a5faae12e4ed952d65aec70f87a7e71f64594f0c61f92.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b},$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{c}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8531da3a-3a08-5b28-869a-8b5faccae9ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572460} {:answer/id #uuid "b081c18c-b180-5eb0-8c11-e10c3e68cddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572510} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d77447-c1f0-57ce-85f8-0d94c6932498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572556} {:answer/id #uuid "e6653684-3eb7-543a-b870-e6c60c1544db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572567} {:answer/id #uuid "faf87587-6680-5ce8-9dce-68c1abd43d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d77447-c1f0-57ce-85f8-0d94c6932498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572556} :answer-field/id #uuid "03796815-dbdc-551f-b90d-8813f015cb09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572589}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95085e88-c005-5feb-bf13-36ae5ea5d897" :question/math-academy-id "q-70144" :question/problem "![](images/69/695c825905b268e40b7c77c681c608fcedfed4d0c5150f76224c4e60eb8ab045.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d388cdd1db5cfe59b447c3fc27b597fc4e42397966935592f56c1c9f4a598e6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef19738-4437-5724-8088-f2b1a053bb1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735026} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8e9c70-fdef-5ab1-a595-6e3efc6f713f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735028} {:answer/id #uuid "72a2ab45-b673-5d6e-90b0-7cc1b3d81003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735032} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9c6273-52a7-57a0-a3ab-332c61a38949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735039} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb5cbaa-5742-58ef-92b5-52201ee77fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b8e9c70-fdef-5ab1-a595-6e3efc6f713f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735028} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5c3d78-052d-5b44-aaaf-5a19e982ffef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735031}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66d86028-4215-5205-b200-ed09b51ad511" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$ on a square grid.\n\n![](images/e7/e781601d7f485104824be4324946dfb9e7adadc23dac64986a35d438f5428aec.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf c=\\lambda\\mathbf a+2\\mathbf b$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "First draw $2\\mathbf b$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Since $\\mathbf b$ goes one square left and two squares up, $2\\mathbf b$ goes two squares left and four squares up.\n\n![](images/81/817a1572fd17f7924f7c4808f04b273ed0ab8ed852368f0f911b40d9b3458b84.png)\n\nThe remaining vector $\\overrightarrow{PC}$ goes six squares right. It has the same direction as $\\mathbf a$, which goes four squares right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{6}{4}\\mathbf a=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a.\n$$\n\nUsing the path from $O$ through $P$ to $C$,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}=2\\mathbf b+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are not parallel, matching their unique coefficients with $\\mathbf c=\\lambda\\mathbf a+2\\mathbf b$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{3}{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7651a6ea-aff0-509f-bfc5-a375145825d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7651a6ea-aff0-509f-bfc5-a375145825d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735033} :answer-field/id #uuid "82083fa6-8670-5d7b-b48c-3d51731bdad6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735034}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4997348-10cf-5382-8f15-1b6111ade6f2" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Solid grid lines are one unit apart; dotted lines divide each square in half.\n\n![](images/48/485299e056823946fcd349f9cab69b45d12127237a192e2869e322e744ed182f.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf c=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a+\\lambda\\mathbf b$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component first. The vector $\\mathbf a$ goes three squares right and two squares up, so $\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a$ goes $4\\frac{1}{2}$ squares right and three squares up. Let $P$ be its endpoint.\n\n![](images/27/27fce7c173b5408cedf468544984f9d779c9b8b608569f530dc0f20e71f1ac06.png)\n\nFrom $P$ to $C$, the remaining displacement is $1\\frac{1}{2}$ squares right and three squares up. Compare this with $\\mathbf b$, which goes two squares right and four squares up:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Horizontal scale} &= \\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{2}=\\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\text{Vertical scale} &= \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth grid displacements have the same positive scale factor. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}$ has the same direction as $\\mathbf b$ and three quarters of its length:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf b.\n$$\n\nThe triangle law now gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf a+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf b.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are not parallel, these coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf b$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{3}{4}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "432a506a-ffc9-5003-92e4-33077e6c36cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735029} {:answer/id #uuid "a1afdb54-75f3-5c34-8c9a-9bf23af434a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735035} {:answer/id #uuid "ad63d400-d2df-539c-bc9f-84e4b2a8a3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735036} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5230da-25ce-5b28-9cbf-05d832a20585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735037} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a9ecaf-b3f0-5554-9e0c-6da6f3797609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "432a506a-ffc9-5003-92e4-33077e6c36cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735029} :answer-field/id #uuid "103eff17-050b-585a-8bf1-92652e5f729c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735027}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f176b311-90b1-5813-81b9-8b06675237d8" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$ on a square grid.\n\n![](images/9c/9c8e6ce5ab61cd32d5173d7dc35c715e54ba53fe5113573bedfe37cc4f2d7bcc.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf c=\\mathbf a+\\lambda\\mathbf b$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "One component is $\\mathbf a$, so begin with $\\overrightarrow{OA}$. The remaining vector is $\\overrightarrow{AC}$.\n\n![](images/a1/a13ee372cbfbea706174ae57c0c78e02ae19d4e469300b23d4ee779f6b815a3e.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf b$ goes up two squares. From $A$ to $C$ we go up six squares, in the same direction. Hence\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\frac{6}{2}\\mathbf b=3\\mathbf b.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf c=\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\mathbf a+3\\mathbf b.\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf b$ gives $\\lambda=3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aeceb4b-9297-5634-b14c-b659e496d52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735385} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa71c5a-ad72-5aca-9a24-8572e035b463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735387} {:answer/id #uuid "af713187-7446-509e-9f13-5b7ff7016b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735390} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc0fa8e-7e27-5c5a-8151-564170920abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735394} {:answer/id #uuid "f95292e0-4711-5fc8-801b-d26b7d72662c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aeceb4b-9297-5634-b14c-b659e496d52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735385} :answer-field/id #uuid "5417a619-c458-5846-a97d-35edcd8a313d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735379}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "764ad590-6274-55b2-ba3a-f46517308ea0" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/02/021a6f546c13d39b564182ed59eb6acb028eb78a8cedb806c7af9ca33a534779.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component $\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, ending at $A$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OA} &=1\\,(5, 0)=(5, 0). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC} &=(0, -4).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/5c/5cd541ffafe7d74e419825c6f244e4583f1bb8040c46f2fd2975f4032078865c.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(0, -4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{-4}{-4}=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AC} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6599b991-1558-5ba1-b641-353caddf656a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6599b991-1558-5ba1-b641-353caddf656a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735382} :answer-field/id #uuid "581ab86e-36a5-5973-8688-054e00a55c9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735380}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5fd80a9-ebec-5fea-9956-08afd7118da5" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/9b/9bc9cd6832996ed599ed7c5fca9ed2c8c6f2772106024f6c1ea0b09c79f3c4b7.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component $\\mathbf{b}$ from $O$, ending at $B$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OB} &=1\\,(0, 2)=(0, 2). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{BC} &=(-6, 0).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b9/b98f2bf570523f9f4ae296b3e321820c6feb3a351fdab7186b60ff60646d11f6.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{a}$, whose grid displacement is $(-2, 0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-6}{-2}=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{BC}=3\\mathbf{a}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OB}+\\overrightarrow{BC} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{a}$ gives $\\lambda=3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07108a27-2cf8-50ed-b0d8-86ac79f089af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735370} {:answer/id #uuid "1df62e1a-4806-53e6-970c-de43741c571a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735374} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec9a9a4-d5d2-53c3-a3cc-f3a37527abb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735376} {:answer/id #uuid "99ea3492-1d0a-59be-8697-827b40a4c880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735388} {:answer/id #uuid "b51f9fda-dba6-5165-8075-be698453033a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ec9a9a4-d5d2-53c3-a3cc-f3a37527abb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735376} :answer-field/id #uuid "696a1cc3-936f-5131-8002-aa7b1cc8f63b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735384}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b085101b-b235-5f7f-b623-4ac3e21615ca" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/fa/fa4ee2495b26b12cb8b8c0ad7f3bcf6713067ef2bf7b6f2d4c3bca9af7b97af1.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component $\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, ending at $A$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OA} &=1\\,(-5, 0)=(-5, 0). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC} &=(0, 6).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/fa/fae0f9f3a7c91dd9aa15bedf707bd50893dc6fff510677f515a9b74250be24f8.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(0, 4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AC} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{3}{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f431bb-ef0d-59ba-a66a-d856ede1aa3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735371} {:answer/id #uuid "13cf6dc4-9c86-5b4b-b280-4a0ab680a761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735373} {:answer/id #uuid "5b627ce2-a19c-5998-be27-bc2860b29252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735381} {:answer/id #uuid "690d8b80-562f-5d90-8b77-3a36f7110105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735383} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d5a7ed-008c-5a5e-bfa7-479f0a352b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13cf6dc4-9c86-5b4b-b280-4a0ab680a761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735373} :answer-field/id #uuid "f445693b-7d8e-587e-8780-f1df4302106f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735398}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd31157d-3178-5399-ab80-97eed38cdf4f" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/6e/6e475a645584c44f974d631bbdbf4e065ad611f1bc4e365a1441adeba95e462a.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component $\\mathbf{b}$ from $O$, ending at $B$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OB} &=1\\,(-5, 0)=(-5, 0). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{BC} &=(0, -7).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ea/ea6e3ab1951e7aebfcce1c3ed7a26696966ba3bb0a7bb8be86e888e062e3e94e.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{a}$, whose grid displacement is $(0, -2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{-7}{-2}=\\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{BC}=\\frac{7}{2}\\mathbf{a}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OB}+\\overrightarrow{BC} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{7}{2}\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{a}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{7}{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6a67a5-cffe-5d7f-b17b-3db22c9b1350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735372} {:answer/id #uuid "226c7bd6-e33a-50da-bc90-aa95327ee923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735375} {:answer/id #uuid "3b54b131-efce-5a23-a565-19e904fd2efc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735377} {:answer/id #uuid "51111ac8-58b1-510a-b7f5-b576b0d4a3f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735378} {:answer/id #uuid "bd70aafb-9bb7-5446-960d-e4216fea7f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "226c7bd6-e33a-50da-bc90-aa95327ee923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735375} :answer-field/id #uuid "f72908b2-4d66-5f34-b4c2-160037912052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735399}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eed86cf8-361a-5183-810e-cdf192a18282" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Grid squares have side length one unit.\n\n![](images/99/99572547820ce2939515e8e93bd4b7b08800f3c94cb8663aa4689b23241a4def.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component $\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, ending at $A$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OA} &=1\\,(2, 0)=(2, 0). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC} &=(0, 4).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/97/97968f79bede28e482151e2bdff3ca5b7d77f445d81cd867c5cfdac573cba435.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(0, 2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{4}{2}=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AC} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735413 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d54211-ff5f-5051-905e-9afef9a388d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19d54211-ff5f-5051-905e-9afef9a388d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735414} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e1907e2-af40-5e56-ba01-fa8d057b4ae3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735415}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0686d6db-4b3e-5c05-9b47-6ef494a7f1e2" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Solid grid lines are one unit apart; dotted lines divide each square in half.\n\n![](images/10/10bc9ef3424bcffdb9fc3958f3885062d2a026d1199477cb2ac3621933560ead.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=\\frac{1}{2}\\,(3, -2)=\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, -1\\right). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=(3, -3).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/94/941dc7de7d4ec7ba07c82bf076a4835dfb79683a5eda4525c20ef5b88e65331c.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(4, -4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{-3}{-4}=\\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{3}{4}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbec857-bdfa-5ce4-93b7-979d192af93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbec857-bdfa-5ce4-93b7-979d192af93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735421} :answer-field/id #uuid "43483cdb-567b-5c55-bded-236793d2adb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735418}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "365903ba-b5ab-557b-8e0c-2efc9ac1761a" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Solid grid lines are one unit apart; dotted lines divide each square in half.\n\n![](images/3f/3f3d535d41f1efaf3cd1ab6af134c8264583281d49007aa1d272b84c5f04fe6c.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component $\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=\\frac{5}{2}\\,(-3, 2)=\\left(-\\frac{15}{2}, 5\\right). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=(-4, 4).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/5d/5d25ca9ee6973a180e4aa36d516544c9f2e1f42c38a44b9c037e9dc4829ceedc.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(-4, 4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-4}{-4}=1 \\\\\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{4}{4}=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc9339a3-4ba8-5fed-81dd-9363559afd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc9339a3-4ba8-5fed-81dd-9363559afd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735424} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e00cee-ce43-5832-a32a-792f28141f58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735422}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43bbfbc7-891c-5b1f-9e1b-23a6a2b10a00" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Solid grid lines are one unit apart; dotted lines divide each square in half.\n\n![](images/38/3876004b0e8b71376f6da2ec1857555cf931c29be5a83dec6b2934ce17f583d1.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=\\frac{1}{2}\\,(-2, 1)=\\left(-1, \\frac{1}{2}\\right). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e2b956ddda0fb60b0ed908451578575f33dce603d78fabd26b1d2ca3dd8f5190.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{a}$, whose grid displacement is $(-2, 2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{-2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{a}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{1}{4}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68b988cb-7858-556b-80dd-e8a008beba0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68b988cb-7858-556b-80dd-e8a008beba0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735420} :answer-field/id #uuid "3711db4a-3e75-56ed-ac3a-e2ab91002150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735417}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2ac4d7e-a633-5182-a775-d21ede953b8c" :question/problem "The arrows show $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf a$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf b$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf c$. Solid grid lines are one unit apart; dotted lines divide each square in half.\n\n![](images/df/dfb02e1d9202130f955625a46621b81e8fdb9ff6c7f66e183577eb13e2ad2775.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c}=\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n$\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Construct the known component $\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ from $O$, and call its endpoint $P$. Count signed horizontal and vertical grid displacements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &=\\frac{5}{2}\\,(-2, 3)=\\left(-5, \\frac{15}{2}\\right). \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PC} &=(-7, 7).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ae/ae434599433e3432485a0a5d2d410c20d7a13e0d16847cb5a6c7f281587e03b2.png)\n\nCompare the remaining displacement with $\\mathbf{b}$, whose grid displacement is $(-4, 4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Horizontal scale} &=\\frac{-7}{-4}=\\frac{7}{4} \\\\\n\\text{Vertical scale} &=\\frac{7}{4}=\\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe scale is positive, so the remaining vector has the same direction as $\\mathbf{b}$. Thus $\\overrightarrow{PC}=\\frac{7}{4}\\mathbf{b}$. By the triangle law,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c} &=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC} \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{7}{4}\\mathbf{b} \\\\\n&=\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{7}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, so their coefficients are unique. Matching the coefficient of $\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=\\frac{7}{4}$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976 :question/id #uuid "aeaa36a2-41b5-5b7b-9797-f39df1e34050" :question/math-academy-id "e-26144" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d4/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} ,$ find $\\lambda.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ So, we first draw the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/65/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $- 2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} .\n$$\n\nIn $△ O P C ,$ we have:\n\n- $O P = 2 | \\mathbf{b} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ and $\\mathbf b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O P} = - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- $P C = | \\mathbf{a} |$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{P C}$ and $\\mathbf a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{P C} = - \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} = - \\mathbf{a} - 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186760607 :question-generator/id #uuid "af5d395d-6f0f-596a-92e5-1a62ca279446" :question-generator/script "generators/61/61c2af93a404592ba4e52ae454bbf307eefacb918697db13dc1dfb6087486e12/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2825307a-aa7d-5547-aea7-e3fd9fc35c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234604} {:answer/id #uuid "31b3d45c-75c3-5b36-bed9-702b6e747092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234623} {:answer/id #uuid "42531acf-e3b1-5ebf-b2e5-355551db3842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234648} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbea967-4af3-5a64-b6ab-5b69a3e7ef69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234786} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b9b3ae-2a50-574c-bc09-ca9bdcb3fe18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cbea967-4af3-5a64-b6ab-5b69a3e7ef69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234786} :answer-field/id #uuid "a701d358-5fb9-58f4-9c4f-33c1160ac672" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235178}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df097f49-5d4b-56bd-b0e8-a7fcc926dc0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120254" :question/problem "![](images/68/6882323d2c8017e95100a0ab9d1091fdf149e884ef27d2d2bf59ab7609b1a960.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambd