ly be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220d1dec-e944-5790-bc26-1f670fabc91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515714} {:answer/id #uuid "25dd4c6a-da11-5495-b232-31ee59481fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515715} {:answer/id #uuid "5214182d-58e0-502d-9eb3-f4444325ea2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515718} {:answer/id #uuid "72540c89-33d7-5fd2-a17a-6bf6fce3a0f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515723} {:answer/id #uuid "c88531be-efec-51d9-bba8-8f694c027453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5214182d-58e0-502d-9eb3-f4444325ea2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515718} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e587eb9-3777-51a4-8ee6-74b29e1d0b17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515722}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce5d8fd6-4de7-54d7-a57c-e25407daa7b1" :question/problem "![](images/35/35b69ac4ee471e78265590690fdf41416bba6b025e5368f0305192a8d5179322.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/71/711f70f4dee6e78ebb691697113de9410df7bc81eba8d9ea65c959120976600d.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}=-2\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fa90b12-e9e3-5071-bca6-826347f8807a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fa90b12-e9e3-5071-bca6-826347f8807a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515717} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ea4a1d-d69b-5669-9edd-886adc6435b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515725}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2d4fc44-21fb-57b5-8495-1ccc49b51a1f" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e9713958d48a4046546fcd2838f37eab41bd0bcca6efc6e204c845d60eac539.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b2/b2f5f00c7c968d98dd37e2e038b65d00b7cf5accb4aaf2bf2b801f15d3a27082.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753351b1-b1b4-570a-92f5-6b28e3bcb89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "753351b1-b1b4-570a-92f5-6b28e3bcb89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735157} :answer-field/id #uuid "06125c00-a990-57d9-b1f2-3cf169598b83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735152}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34fbadff-3a9c-55e2-b760-fde7afe5ad17" :question/problem "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/8e/8e6ccf522b5325c3b592ca9470810a95c5491f0b68f000ea774f6555cee0f47f.png)\n\nIf $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The known component is $-2\\mathbf{b}$: reverse the direction of $\\mathbf{b}$ and double its length. Starting at the common origin $O$, this takes us $4$ squares right and $4$ squares down to a point $P$.\n\n![](images/64/646b8f1322f815e786f82eb8cfb3fa9e84a3c8d588c94910b0b702b607bfb2d8.png)\n\nTo reach the endpoint $C$ of $\\mathbf{c}$ from $P$, move $6$ squares up. But $\\mathbf{a}$ runs $4$ squares down. Therefore the remaining vector has the opposite direction and $\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}$ times the length of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nAdding the path vectors,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=-2\\mathbf{b}-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, matching coefficients gives $\\lambda=-\\frac{3}{2}$. The ratio compares the remaining path with $\\mathbf{a}$, not with $\\mathbf{b}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fb29e9-eef4-5772-aa39-61aa0430a9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735153} {:answer/id #uuid "87dbedab-85ef-5430-92cd-7b2aca73afb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735158} {:answer/id #uuid "c47ec203-049d-5283-a4cb-ba1f7dcd856c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735161} {:answer/id #uuid "f199399a-b25d-5545-8718-5b1b1524aa90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735166} {:answer/id #uuid "fe30a522-a2c4-5b1b-9ce9-c81502398835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30fb29e9-eef4-5772-aa39-61aa0430a9a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735153} :answer-field/id #uuid "e931a417-27b2-5bad-a518-9d6060dcc04a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735165}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e4b87c8-6b40-5e31-8fe4-02d1a12ae9c0" :question/problem "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/68/6882323d2c8017e95100a0ab9d1091fdf149e884ef27d2d2bf59ab7609b1a960.png)\n\nGiven $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "First trace $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the shared origin $O$ and call its endpoint $P$. The remaining vector from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ of $\\mathbf{c}$ must be $\\lambda\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/a8/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}$ runs $4$ grid squares downward. The remaining path $\\overrightarrow{PC}$ runs $8$ squares upward, so it is twice as long and points in the opposite direction:\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-2\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nThus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}\\\\\n&=-2\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, so comparing their unique coefficients gives $\\lambda=-2$. A positive $2$ would have the correct length but the wrong direction."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aa994c2-bcfc-5791-a769-342433a7b79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735156} {:answer/id #uuid "b7082c37-47c2-552b-a087-84de944fbd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735159} {:answer/id #uuid "becf6093-e6a4-5386-9d97-c5091f912711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735160} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb86a3d-931a-5a34-8280-b5df9fde65d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735162} {:answer/id #uuid "f4defb24-c6ff-58c9-9a93-0e2a0e20f7c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7082c37-47c2-552b-a087-84de944fbd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735159} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37c4fcd-9300-55be-8402-a8c0b2260c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735163}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d874f13e-2944-5efe-8d02-d18e0d11d347" :question/problem "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/af/afdd59dbd0050ed655b319b015b2ef03de3c8a314b8ba5e9c29c5755be0a4b66.png)\n\nGiven $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Draw $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$, then complete the path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ of $\\mathbf{c}$.\n\n![](images/ce/cee2a501a390c40bc1100336ff4a7cda3e7a070dff7dc0470b1762833a7a677a.png)\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{PC}$ points left, while $\\mathbf{a}$ points right. Their lengths are equal, so\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nAdding the two path vectors gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}\\\\\n&=-\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, their coefficients are unique. Comparing with $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=-1$. The minus sign records direction; the equal lengths give magnitude $1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c45d3b2-3912-50bd-a92b-c74dfba6cd98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735580} {:answer/id #uuid "7f14bcfb-70b5-568a-994f-1e07ba4cdf6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735582} {:answer/id #uuid "a21c5c29-c772-5304-933b-cfef9270c645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735586} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7daef8-2732-5c5c-a379-b84b6c2d1073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735591} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a73ed3-b7f0-5f16-94de-e8d306003558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c45d3b2-3912-50bd-a92b-c74dfba6cd98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735580} :answer-field/id #uuid "4195611b-a137-55a2-aece-a197a64444e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21bed016-981c-5a78-b925-a89677b349ad" :question/problem "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/30/304e0adaf1219a49e256ffed030701f793b975bfcf8efb17c519b6e10eface9d.png)\n\nGiven $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "First trace $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$: keep its direction and scale its length. The remaining path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ must be the unknown multiple of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/54/549775db5d66d443394d386f4d1918cab7f5ba9e121baac177be4c7778ef48e1.png)\n\nFollowing the grid direction of $\\mathbf{a}$ takes $3$ steps, while the parallel path $PC$ has $3$ steps. Their length ratio is $\\frac{3}{3}=1$. The remaining path points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$, so\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nAdding the path vectors gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf{b}+(-\\mathbf{a})\\\\\n&=-\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, these coefficients are unique. Comparing with $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=-1$. The minus sign records the reversed direction."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "167e83a2-5685-5128-b651-5614737f964c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735568} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5c2628-b2c5-571c-bd98-d7bc1853bc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735584} {:answer/id #uuid "9690afcc-a846-5597-9623-10b2bcf66d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735585} {:answer/id #uuid "aedfd16e-5148-50e8-87a7-b1df09a073a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735588} {:answer/id #uuid "e7546f93-d97e-55bc-a851-6af6c535efdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "167e83a2-5685-5128-b651-5614737f964c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735568} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b70ce2e-4677-5629-b0fe-2447d0119173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735574}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30312cca-a3ec-548e-8ff9-ed018b1f0650" :question/problem "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/07/072fdd66978bd3d1c50b6979c4e5fbcf63bdd01b16e06b1cfb325e41d706c2cf.png)\n\nGiven $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "First trace $4\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$: keep its direction and scale its length. The remaining path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ must be the unknown multiple of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/38/388f5ac51d455964d4e91e103e6760f097214a0eac64b9296418c14513fd9245.png)\n\nFollowing the grid direction of $\\mathbf{a}$ takes $2$ steps, while the parallel path $PC$ has $6$ steps. Their length ratio is $\\frac{6}{2}=3$. The remaining path points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$, so\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-3\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nAdding the path vectors gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=4\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a})\\\\\n&=-3\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, these coefficients are unique. Comparing with $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=-3$. The minus sign records the reversed direction."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76d402cb-64d0-5d48-bf23-6c617039d57c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76d402cb-64d0-5d48-bf23-6c617039d57c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735576} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f584a4-5cba-5634-90d7-f6106e9ab898" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735592}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f04bf5a-c094-5128-84ab-2a028aa485de" :question/problem "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/6a/6a7bb568076c6353da82a9b284164db4df8b4b0ea93510ef26e8d8a9953031c2.png)\n\nIf $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First trace $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$: keep its direction and scale its length. The remaining path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ must be the unknown multiple of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/68/689f23204908654ec356b268778934b5f828b6ac966bc80ea987c38740ff79b2.png)\n\nFollowing the grid direction of $\\mathbf{a}$ takes $4$ steps, while the parallel path $PC$ has $12$ steps. Their length ratio is $\\frac{12}{4}=3$. The remaining path points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$, so\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-3\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nAdding the path vectors gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{b}+(-3\\mathbf{a})\\\\\n&=-3\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, these coefficients are unique. Comparing with $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=-3$. The minus sign records the reversed direction."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3ab19ca-3b67-5a4d-900c-6da6b2e41042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3ab19ca-3b67-5a4d-900c-6da6b2e41042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735589} :answer-field/id #uuid "872bb91e-8087-5b8e-a0ac-e1128111d0b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735583}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d4d54c1-806a-52f7-9478-47fc2534694c" :question/problem "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/35/3586dbaa50e66bf8725551e146b9c50ee30745d1d9375354ce49584add8458bc.png)\n\nIf $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\lambda=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First trace $4\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$: keep its direction and scale its length. The remaining path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ mu