} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186735571}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9c0472b-e065-532b-bd6d-161575c56099" :question/problem "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/4e/4e0556f4e4b84d5bd80916517088596ca4ff887e6b58992567855da0bc948525.png)\n\nGiven $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "First trace $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$: keep its direction and scale its length. The remaining path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ must be the unknown multiple of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/ed/edd0d6d9f3b0331cb81032440dc33713c1780a50f77d4b040f67e2ad2028fdb2.png)\n\nFollowing the grid direction of $\\mathbf{a}$ takes $4$ steps, while the parallel path $PC$ has $16$ steps. Their length ratio is $\\frac{16}{4}=4$. The remaining path points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$, so\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-4\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nAdding the path vectors gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{b}+(-4\\mathbf{a})\\\\\n&=-4\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, these coefficients are unique. Comparing with $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=-4$. The minus sign records the reversed direction."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}]}
